---

[Webpack](https://webpack.js.org/) is a static module bundler for modern JavaScript applications. In a Webpack monorepo, Nx brings smart task running and caching to every project.

The `@nx/webpack` plugin provides executors that allow you to build and serve your projects using webpack, plus an executor for SSR.

You can [customize your webpack configuration](/docs/kb/webpack-config-setup) for your projects. Nx also provides [a number of webpack plugins](/docs/kb/webpack-plugins) for supporting Nx and other frameworks.

## Requirements

The `@nx/webpack` plugin supports the following package versions.

| Package              | Supported Versions             |
| -------------------- | ------------------------------ |
| `webpack`            | ^5.0.0                         |
| `webpack-dev-server` | ^5.0.0                         |
| `webpack-cli`        | ^5.0.0 \|\| ^6.0.0 \|\| ^7.0.0 |

[Nx generators](/docs/features/generate-code) install the latest supported versions automatically when scaffolding new projects.

## Setting up a new Nx workspace with Webpack

You can create a new workspace that uses Webpack with one of the following commands:

- Generate a new standalone React app set up with Webpack

```shell
npx create-nx-workspace@latest --preset=react-standalone --bundler=webpack
```

- Generate a new React monorepo set up with Webpack

```shell
npx create-nx-workspace@latest --preset=react-monorepo --bundler=webpack
```

### Installation

{% aside type="note" title="Keep Nx Package Versions In Sync" %}
Make sure to install the `@nx/webpack` version that matches the version of `nx` in your repository. If the version numbers get out of sync, you can encounter some difficult to debug errors. You can [fix Nx version mismatches with this recipe](/docs/kb/keep-nx-versions-in-sync).
{% /aside %}

In any Nx workspace, you can install `@nx/webpack` by running the following command:

```shell
nx add @nx/webpack
```

This will install the correct version of `@nx/webpack`.

### How @nx/webpack infers tasks

The `@nx/webpack` plugin will create a task for any project that has a Webpack configuration file present. Any of the following files will be recognized as a Webpack configuration file:

- `webpack.config.js`
- `webpack.config.ts`
- `webpack.config.mjs`
- `webpack.config.cjs`

The configuration directory must also contain a `package.json` or `project.json`.

### View inferred tasks

To view inferred tasks for a project, open the [project details view](/docs/concepts/mental-model#inferred-tasks) in Nx Console or run `nx show project my-project --web` in the command line.

### @nx/webpack configuration

The `@nx/webpack/plugin` is configured in the `plugins` array in `nx.json`.

```json {% meta="{2-10}" %}
// nx.json
{
  "plugins": [
    {
      "plugin": "@nx/webpack/plugin",
      "options": {
        "buildTargetName": "build",
        "previewTargetName": "preview",
        "serveTargetName": "serve",
        "serveStaticTargetName": "serve-static",
        "buildDepsTargetName": "build-deps",
        "watchDepsTargetName": "watch-deps"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

| Option                  | Default        | Inferred behavior                                                |
| ----------------------- | -------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `buildTargetName`       | `build`        | Runs and caches `webpack-cli build` with its configured outputs. |
| `serveTargetName`       | `serve`        | Runs `webpack-cli serve` continuously in development mode.       |
| `previewTargetName`     | `preview`      | Runs `webpack-cli serve` continuously in production mode.        |
| `serveStaticTargetName` | `serve-static` | Serves the build output continuously after the build task.       |
| `buildDepsTargetName`   | `build-deps`   | Name of the dependency build task.                               |
| `watchDepsTargetName`   | `watch-deps`   | Name of the dependency watch task.                               |

The build task depends on builds of project dependencies. When TypeScript project references are enabled, the tasks also run the TypeScript sync generator.

The `serve` and `preview` tasks are [ready](/docs/reference/project-configuration#wait-for-a-continuous-task-to-be-ready) once the dev server accepts connections, and a task that depends on them with `waitFor: "ready"` waits until then.
Nx checks this address:

- Port: the `--port` value, otherwise `devServer.port` from the Webpack configuration, otherwise the `WEBPACK_DEV_SERVER_BASE_PORT` environment variable, otherwise `8080`. A `devServer.port` of `auto` counts as unset.
- Host: the `--host` value, otherwise `devServer.host`, otherwise `localhost`.

With several configurations that set `devServer`, Nx reads the last one, because webpack-cli starts its dev server only after the others accept connections.

When the port is `0`, or you pass `--port auto`, the dev server picks its port when it starts, so Nx skips the check.
A task that waits for `serve` or `preview` then runs once they start.

When the configuration sets `devServer.ipc`, or the port isn't a number when Nx builds the project graph, Nx waits for the dev server to log its address instead.
The port isn't a number when the configuration computes it from an environment variable that is unset at that point.
For example, `port: Number(process.env.APP_PORT)` gives `NaN` when `APP_PORT` is set only in the project's `.env` file, because Nx loads that file only for tasks.
Nx skips this check, and a waiting task runs once the dev server starts, in these cases:

- `infrastructureLogging.level` is `none`, `error`, or `warn`, or `infrastructureLogging` sets its own `console` or `stream`.
- Several configurations set `devServer`. Each dev server logs its address, so the first line comes before the last one accepts connections.

The `serve-static` task is ready once the file server accepts connections on its `host` and `port` options.

When the port Nx checks is taken, the file server moves to the next free port.
The dev server does the same when the port is unset or `devServer.port` is `auto`.
Nx keeps checking the original port, so the process already listening there counts as ready.

Nx checks the wrong port, or waits for a line the dev server never logs, in these cases:

- The configuration reads the port from an environment variable, such as `port: process.env.APP_PORT` or `port: Number(process.env.APP_PORT) || 4200`. Nx reads the configuration when it builds the project graph and caches the result. It doesn't see a value that changes between runs, or one that only the task's environment sets, such as the project's `.env` file or the target's `env` option.
- A Webpack plugin changes `devServer.port`. Nx reads the configuration without running its plugins.
- The port comes from a `--port` flag inside the `args` option, such as `"args": ["--port=4300"]`. Nx follows the `port` option, which `args` doesn't set. Pass the port as the `port` option or as `--port` on the command line instead.
- You select a configuration with `--config-name`, and it isn't the last one that sets `devServer`. Nx still checks the port of the last one.
- Nx waits for the dev server to log its address, and the `--infrastructure-logging-level` flag or a Webpack plugin that intercepts infrastructure logging hides that line.

When another task waits for that check, Nx fails the dev server task after 60 seconds, and the waiting task doesn't run.
To fix it, set `readyWhen` yourself.
It replaces the inferred check, and `null` turns the check off.
To set it for every Webpack project, use a target default:

```json
// nx.json
{
  "targetDefaults": {
    "serve": [
      {
        "filter": { "plugin": "@nx/webpack/plugin" },
        "readyWhen": { "port": "{env.APP_PORT ?? 4200}" }
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

A `readyWhen` on the target in `project.json` takes precedence over target defaults.

Use `include` and `exclude` glob patterns on the plugin entry to scope inference. A [target defaults](/docs/reference/nx-json#target-defaults) `plugin` filter must use the exact identifier `@nx/webpack/plugin`.

## Generate a new project using Webpack

You can generate a [React](/docs/technologies/react/introduction) application or a [Web](/docs/reference/web) application that uses Webpack in an existing Nx workspace. The [`@nx/react:app`](/docs/technologies/react/generators#application), [`@nx/node:app`](/docs/technologies/node/generators#application) and [`@nx/web:app`](/docs/reference/web/generators#application) generators accept the `bundler` option, where you can pass `webpack`. This will generate a new application configured to use Webpack, and it will also install all the necessary dependencies, including the `@nx/webpack` plugin.

To generate a React application using Webpack, run the following:

```bash {% frame="none" %}
nx g @nx/react:app apps/my-app --bundler=webpack
```

To generate a Node application using Webpack, run the following:

```bash {% frame="none" %}
nx g @nx/node:app apps/my-app --bundler=webpack
```

To generate a Web application using Webpack, run the following:

```bash {% frame="none" %}
nx g @nx/web:app apps/my-app --bundler=webpack
```

## Set up CI for your Webpack monorepo

In CI, Nx runs [`nx affected`](/docs/features/ci-features/affected) to rebuild and retest only the projects a change touches, and [caches](/docs/features/cache-task-results) results to skip repeated work.

For a complete pipeline, see [Set up CI](/docs/getting-started/setup-ci).
